From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Don't wait for the PS5 desktop window's owner.

A game that sent a message to the desktop window could stop forever: a
frozen picture with almost no CPU in use and no crash (The Evil Within).

On a desktop Wine, explorer owns the desktop window and reads its queue.
On the PS5 the server creates that window and no process serves it. The
window's handle entry still names the first thread that asked for it,
so a message sent from any other thread goes to that thread's queue and
the sender waits for a reply. If that thread never reads its queue, the
reply never comes. The Evil Within's main thread only waits for the game
thread, and the game thread calls InvalidateRect(NULL, NULL, TRUE), whose
RDW_ERASENOW sends WM_ERASEBKGND to the desktop.

If that thread does read its queue the reply is always 0, because this
process holds no WND for the window: call_window_proc() and
init_window_call_params() refuse it, so the desktop's window procedure
never runs here. A sender on another thread now gets that 0 at once. The
same goes for the message-only window, whose procedure also answers 0.

This holds for every message that waits for a result, such as the
WM_DISPLAYCHANGE from a display mode change. Notify and callback messages
do not wait and stay as they were, as does a send from the named thread.
A WH_CALLWNDPROC hook on the named thread no longer sees these sends.
---

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/message.c b/dlls/win32u/message.c
index 8c9a2aa..734b72d 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/message.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/message.c
@@ -4422,6 +4422,24 @@ static BOOL process_message( struct send_message_info *info, DWORD_PTR *res_ptr,
     if (!(info->dest_tid = get_window_thread( info->hwnd, &dest_pid ))) return FALSE;
     if (is_exiting_thread( info->dest_tid )) return FALSE;
 
+#ifdef WINE_PS5_USER_DRIVER
+    /* no explorer serves the server-created desktop: its handle still names the
+     * thread that first asked for it, which may never read its queue. That thread
+     * could only answer 0, as this process holds no WND for the window, so a
+     * sender gets the 0 now. A new thread hasn't yet cached the handles that
+     * is_desktop_window() reads. */
+    if (info->dest_tid != GetCurrentThreadId() &&
+        (info->type == MSG_ASCII || info->type == MSG_UNICODE || info->type == MSG_OTHER_PROCESS))
+    {
+        cache_thread_desktop_windows();
+        if (is_desktop_window( info->hwnd ))
+        {
+            if (res_ptr) *res_ptr = 0;
+            return TRUE;
+        }
+    }
+#endif
+
     if (info->params && info->dest_tid == GetCurrentThreadId() &&
         !is_hooked( WH_CALLWNDPROC ) && !is_hooked( WH_CALLWNDPROCRET ) &&
         thread_info->msg_call_depth <= MAX_WINPROC_RECURSION)
-- 
